<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Udabhandapura</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hund</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Udabhandapura, modern Hund in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</a> province, was a city on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attock">Attock</a>, at a main crossing of the river. It became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Shahi kings after they lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in the late 9th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> is thought to have crossed the Indus nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/udabhandapura.jpg" alt="Udabhandapura" width="320" height="677" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shehrology · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Great_Alexander%27s_Memorial_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
